ultaneous connections and aggregate bandwidth across multiple frequency bands and channels [Data: Entities (1040)]. This capability leads to a significant increase in throughput and enhances link reliability and resilience, thereby reducing latency [Data: Entities (1040)]. The benefits of MLO are substantial, including dramatically improved data transfer rates, greater network reliability, and increased resilience against interference or link failures [Data: Entities (1040)]. These advancements are crucial for demanding applications such as immersive experiences and industrial operations [Data: Entities (1040)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Interdependence of IEEE 802.11be and MLO\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The IEEE 802.11be standard is intrinsically linked to Multi-Link Operation (MLO) [Data: Relationships (1645)]. MLO is not merely an additional feature but a core technology designed to deliver the performance gains promised by Wi-Fi 7 [Data: Relationships (1645)]. The standard explicitly introduces MLO to enable devices to connect and transmit data across multiple Wi-Fi bands and links concurrently, aiming to boost throughput, lower latency, and enhance overall network reliability [Data: Relationships (1645)]. This synergy between the standard and its enabling technology is critical for achieving higher data rates and maintaining stable connections in challenging radio frequency environments [Data: Relationships (1645)]. MLO is indeed a fundamental feature of the 802.11be standard [Data: Relationships (1613)].\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Relationship between Wi-Fi 7 and IEEE 802.11be\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Wi-Fi 7 serves as the commercial designation for the IEEE 802.11be standard [Data: Entities (1043)]. This relationship highlights the practical application and market branding of the technical specifications defined by the IEEE standard [Data: Relationships (1648)]. While IEEE 802.11be refers to the technical protocol, Wi-Fi 7 is th